Mom’s hands smooth the front of my jacket flat, once, twice, tugging the hem down over my shorts until it hangs straight. Then she slides one arm under my knees and lifts me onto her hip, settling me there like a bag she’s carried a hundred times.

“No more wandering,” she says into my hair. “You stay up here with me.”

I don’t argue. I tuck my elbow against her ribs and my hand goes back flat over the pocket. My cheek ends up on her shoulder, and from there I can see Dad coming across the grass at a hard run, arms pumping, with his sandals kicking up little clods of dirt. Ichigo is a few steps behind with his arms crossed, trying to look like he isn’t running.

“Anzu!” Dad skids to a stop so close that Mom’s hair flutters. He drops into a squat anyway and looks me over, up and down and sideways. The loud goes out of his face for a moment. He takes my wrist in both hands, turns it over, and runs a thumb along the scratches. “Hm. Hm-hm. Shallow. Nothing deep.” His fingers press at my elbow, then each knuckle, then he tips my chin side to side to check my eyes. “Any dizziness? Does your head hurt?”

I shake my head.

“Good girl.” He lifts my hand into the light. “Dirt in all of it, though. Masaki, the water?”

“Already out,” Mom says. She digs in her bag with her free hand, comes up with the bottle and a packet of wipes, and Dad takes my arm and holds it steady, palm up, like it’s something he’s about to put on a shelf. She tears the packet with her teeth.

The first wipe is cold. Then it isn’t cold at all. It stings so sharply that my whole arm jerks inside Dad’s grip, and my shoulder rises toward my ear. My face does nothing. I stare at the swings.

“I know, I know,” Mom murmurs, wiping slower. “Almost done. You’re being so brave.”

“She’s not even crying,” Ichigo says. He’s come up beside Dad with his hands jammed in his pockets. “She just fell in some bushes. It’s not a big deal.”

“Ichigo, a little kindness toward your sister,” Dad says. He’s still bent over my arm, but his eyes have gone somewhere else. Over my shoulder, toward the far side of the field, to the old plane tree. They sit there for about as long as it takes to blink, flat and quiet. Then he turns his head back to my hand, grins wide, and says, “Brave! Isn’t that right? A true Kurosaki! The doctor is very proud!”

“Isshin, your voice,” Mom says, still dabbing.

“Her ears are fine, she’s a patient, I’m allowed to—”

“Daaad.” Karin has hold of his belt and is hanging off it, dragging her sneakers through the dirt. “Can we go? I’m hungry. You said snacks.”

“Anzu’s bleeding,” Yuzu says. She’s standing behind Karin with both hands pressed to her mouth, and her eyes have already gone shiny. “Is she gonna be okay? Mama, is Anzu gonna be okay?”

“She’s fine, sweetheart, it’s just scratches.” Mom turns my arm toward her, tapping a last wipe against my wrist. “See? All clean.”

Dad pinches something out from under my thumbnail with two fingers before I can pull the hand away. “Splinter,” he says, and drops it into the dirt. “There. Now we’re done.”

“Hey,” Ichigo says, scowling at the ground between his sandals. “Let’s just go home.”

“Home it is!” Dad scoops Yuzu up in one arm without even asking her, and she squeals, then hiccups. Karin climbs onto his back, hooking her arms around his neck and her knees under his elbows. He staggers and groans like they weigh a hundred kilos. “Ow! My spine! My poor old spine!”

“You’re not even old,” Karin says, flatly, right by his ear.

We set off down the path. Mom’s hip rocks under me with every step, and the sun is low enough now to hit my eyes whenever I lift my head. Ichigo walks on her other side, kicking a pebble ahead of him and stepping over it each time. Dad starts whistling something off-key. Behind us, at the far end of the park, the plane tree sticks up over the grass with its pale strip of bark still hanging down.

I keep my hand where it is.
